                <pb facs="1056" n="928" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg928"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 701.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Scott</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-12-04"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 4,
                        1885</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 35.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The consul of the United States at
                    Maracaibo, in a dispatch of the 30th October last, calls attention to the
                    embarrassments occasioned to American shipping by the practices prevailing at
                    the custom-house there regarding ships’ papers. He incloses a copy of his No.
                    20, of May 15,1879, making suggestions touching the prevision of a remedy
                    forthese embarrassments.</p>
                <p>I inclose a copy of the original dispatch, and have to ask that you will solicit
                    at the foreign office the changes in the customs regulations there suggested by
                    the consul.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>T. F. BAYARD.</signed></closer>




